# The shipped CLI binaries must never be touched by line-ending conversion.
#
# core.autocrlf is true on the maintainer's machine, and without this file
# git decides "is it binary?" by looking for NUL bytes in the first 8 KB. It
# gets it right for these files today -- but a heuristic is the wrong thing
# to stake a user's install on, and the Linux and macOS binaries carry no
# .exe to hint with. A single CRLF rewrite inside an ELF header produces a
# file that still looks fine in `git status` and fails on someone else's
# machine with no diagnosis available from here.
#
# The binaries are named, rather than globbing bin/** and exempting the
# README: `binary` is a macro for `-text -diff`, and clawing `diff` back
# afterwards is easy to get subtly wrong. Naming them leaves every text
# file in bin/ completely untouched.
bin/**/ringpp     binary
bin/**/ringpp.exe binary

# Vendored third-party sources check out BYTE-EXACT, never line-ending
# converted. These are someone else's release artefacts and the project makes
# provenance claims about them -- upstream/tree-sitter-ring-notes.md quotes
# sha cb5bb1e7 for v1.1.1's parser.c and invites the maintainer to check it.
#
# Without this, core.autocrlf rewrote parser.c on checkout from 21,848,802
# bytes to 22,412,612, so the working copy no longer matched the release it
# claimed to be. Git had stored the file correctly; only the checkout was
# wrong, which is the version anyone would actually compare.
vendor/** -text

# Image assets: same reasoning as the CLI binaries above, extended before
# it was needed rather than after a corrupted checkout was found. JPEGs
# and PNGs are binary-heavy enough that git's heuristic almost always
# gets this right on its own -- "almost always" is exactly the standard
# this file exists to not rely on.
*.jpg binary
*.png binary
